---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ActionLabel from '../../components/ActionLabel.astro';
import '../../styles/guides-page.css';

const title = 'Your cloud bill grew. What changed?';
const description = 'Use Cloud Cost Guard to review a billing CSV, compare two periods, and choose what to check next. A walkthrough with two downloadable example bills.';
const articleUrl = 'https://cloudandcapital.com/insights/using-cloud-cost-guard';
const articleData = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `${articleUrl}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#diana' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#organization' },
  mainEntityOfPage: articleUrl,
  datePublished: '2026-10-23',
  dateModified: '2026-10-23',
  image: 'https://cloudandcapital.com/images/cloud-cost-guard-billing-preview.jpg',
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Cloud & Capital`} description={description} type="article" structuredData={[articleData]}>
  <main id="main-content" class="guides-page guard-guide">
    <article class="guide-shell guide-article">
      <header>
        <a class="guide-back" href="/insights">← Guides &amp; writing</a>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Cloud bills · A tool walkthrough</p>
        <h1><span>Your cloud bill grew.</span> <em>What changed?</em></h1>
        <p class="guide-byline">By Diana Molski · <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time></p>
        <p class="guide-lede">A bigger bill tells you something changed. It doesn’t tell you where to start. Cloud Cost Guard breaks down your billing records and compares periods, so you can see which services deserve a closer look.</p>
        <p>Bring one supported billing CSV for a spending breakdown. Add an earlier bill to see what moved. Let’s try it with two small, made-up bills first.</p>
        <a class="guide-button" href="https://guard.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="cloud_cost_guard" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Open Cloud Cost Guard" /></a>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="example-title">
        <h2 id="example-title">Compare two bills with me.</h2>
        <p>These files contain invented AWS charges for August and September 2026. They’re here so you can follow along before using your own records.</p>
        <div class="guard-example-files">
          <p class="guide-eyebrow">The two example bills</p>
          <a href="/examples/cloud-cost-guard/current-example.csv" download="cloud-cost-guard-september-example.csv">Download September CSV <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
          <a href="/examples/cloud-cost-guard/previous-example.csv" download="cloud-cost-guard-august-example.csv">Download August CSV <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
        <ol class="guard-steps">
          <li>Open the tool. Under <strong>Your current bill</strong>, choose the September file.</li>
          <li>Under <strong>An earlier bill</strong>, choose the August file.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Review my bill</strong>. The first review prepares the analysis tools on your device, so give it a moment.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>For this walkthrough, use the files above. The tool’s separate <strong>Try an example</strong> button opens a different sample dashboard.</p>
        <table class="guard-example-table">
          <caption>What the uploaded example shows</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col">Days</th><th scope="col">Net cost</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">August</th><td>31</td><td>$996.60</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">September</th><td>30</td><td>$1,310.13</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The increase is <strong>$313.53, or 31.5%</strong>. Those are changes in the supplied period totals, rounded for display. August and September have different numbers of days, so this isn’t a comparison of daily spending rates.</p>
      </section>

      <figure class="guide-screenshot guard-wide-screenshot">
        <img src="/images/cloud-cost-guard-billing-preview.jpg" width="1232" height="541" alt="The actual uploaded example: $1,310.13 net cloud cost, $1,362.38 in positive charges, minus $52.25 in credits, with charges grouped by service" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>September’s made-up bill in the live tool. Credits reduce the total; the chart shows positive charges by service.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section aria-labelledby="next-title">
        <h2 id="next-title">Start with the biggest change.</h2>
        <p>Open <strong>Findings</strong> and look at <strong>Biggest movement</strong> and <strong>Largest changes</strong>. In our example, API compute increased by <strong>$240.03</strong>. That’s where I’d start looking.</p>
        <p>I’d ask the service owner what changed between the periods, then check usage, rates, and the underlying charges. Was there more demand? A different configuration? A price change? The service total gives us a place to investigate. It doesn’t answer those questions on its own.</p>
        <p>Keep the next step small: one service, someone who can explain it, and the records needed to check the explanation. A higher bill might make sense for the work being done.</p>
        <aside class="guide-example"><p><strong>“Service totals add up” checks the calculation.</strong> It doesn’t prove every account or charge was included. Compare the upload’s coverage and total with your provider bill before treating it as the full picture.</p></aside>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="own-bill-title">
        <h2 id="own-bill-title">Now try your own bill.</h2>
        <p>Choose <strong>Upload another bill</strong>. The tool accepts supported FOCUS billing CSVs or its simple template. Open <strong>What can I upload?</strong> on the start screen for the format and AWS/Azure export instructions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Use one complete export run per period.</strong> Select all its CSV parts together. Keep the accounts, currency, and cost basis consistent for a comparison, and use a separate earlier period.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep credits and taxes in the records.</strong> In the simple template, the required columns are Date, Service, Cost, and Currency. Credits use negative amounts.</li>
          <li><strong>Check the file type.</strong> PDFs, Excel files, compressed archives, and other providers’ CSV layouts need conversion first. Each period allows up to 10 MB and 50,000 rows.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If the tool asks about identical rows, check whether they’re repeated exports or separate charges before choosing how to count them. A partial export will give you a partial total.</p>
        <p>The uploaded report covers the cloud billing records you supply. Separate software invoices, direct AI bills, or Kubernetes utilization need additional data; those sections explain what’s missing.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="keep-title">
        <h2 id="keep-title">Ask a question. Keep the review.</h2>
        <p>Open <strong>Lumen</strong> and choose <strong>What changed?</strong> for a plain-language explanation. For uploaded bills, Lumen uses prepared answers from your report on your device, covering totals, services, credits, dates, and findings.</p>
        <p>Choose <strong>Printable report</strong> to download an HTML copy you can open in your browser. The CSV exports keep the normalized records, and <strong>Evidence JSON</strong> keeps the fuller report data. Save what you need before leaving.</p>
        <p>Your uploaded records, results, and Lumen questions stay in this tab’s memory. Reloading, closing the tab, or clearing the data removes them. The separate sample dashboard’s AI chat has its own disclosure about sending messages to an AI service.</p>
      </section>

      <footer class="guide-next">
        <h2>Give the change a next step.</h2>
        <p>Use the tool to find where the spending moved, then check the explanation with the person who knows the service. If you’d like help reviewing the records, usage, and terms together, that’s what a Technology Cost Review is for.</p>
        <div class="guide-actions">
          <a class="guide-button" href="https://guard.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="cloud_cost_guard" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Review your cloud bill" /></a>
          <a href="/services#technology-cost-review" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="services" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Explore a cost review" /></a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .guard-guide h1 { max-width:none; }
  .guard-guide h1 > :is(span,em) { display:block; }
  .guard-guide h1 em { color:var(--clay-ink); font-style:italic; }
  .guard-guide header > p + p { margin-top:.8rem; }
  .guard-guide .guide-byline { margin-bottom:1rem; }
  .guard-guide .guide-button { color:white; background:var(--brand-green); border-color:var(--brand-green); }
  .guard-guide .guide-button :global(.action-label) { color:inherit; }
  .guard-guide .guide-screenshot img { padding:0; border:1px solid #d3c9c0; border-radius:12px; background:var(--cream); }
  .guard-example-files { margin:1.5rem 0; padding:1.2rem 1.4rem; border-block:1px solid #c8d1c0; background:var(--sage-surface); }
  .guard-example-files a { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:.5rem; min-height:44px; width:fit-content; max-width:100%; color:var(--brand-green); }
  .guard-example-files a > span { flex:none; }
  .guard-steps { padding-left:1.4rem; }
  .guard-example-table { width:100%; margin:1.75rem 0 1rem; border-collapse:collapse; font-variant-numeric:lining-nums tabular-nums; }
  .guard-example-table caption { padding-bottom:.8rem; text-align:left; font-weight:500; }
  .guard-example-table th,.guard-example-table td { padding:.9rem .7rem; border-bottom:1px solid #d3c9c0; text-align:left; }
  .guard-example-table thead th { color:var(--brand-green); border-top:1px solid #d3c9c0; font-weight:500; }
  .guard-example-table tbody th { font-weight:400; }
  .guard-example-table :is(th,td):last-child { text-align:right; white-space:nowrap; }
  @media(max-width:760px) { .guard-wide-screenshot { display:none; } .guard-example-files { padding:1rem; } .guard-example-table th,.guard-example-table td { padding:.8rem .4rem; font-size:.94rem; } }
</style>
